<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#iroquoian-languages">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iroquoian languages</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iroquoian languages are a family of Native American languages of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The northern branch includes the languages of the Five Nations of the Iroquois Confederacy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a>, Cayuga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, together with Tuscarora, Huron-Wendat and others, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> forms the southern branch. Several are still spoken in communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> and North Carolina, and revival programmes support them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iroquoian-languages.jpg" alt="Iroquoian languages" width="320" height="541" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noahedits · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iroquoian_map_close_up.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Iroquoian languages, occasionally called the Ogwehoweh languages, are a family of indigenous languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The family divides into Northern Iroquoian and Southern Iroquoian, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>. Northern Iroquoian includes the Iroquois Proper or Five Nations group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, Cayuga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a> group, the Huronian languages Wendat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petun">Petun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wyandot">Wyandot</a>, and the Tuscarora–Nottoway group of Tuscarora, Meherrin and Nottoway. Susquehannock, Wenro, Neutral, Erie and Laurentian are unclassified. Cherokee has dialects in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, in North Carolina and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>. Several of these languages are extinct or dormant, including Wendat, Petun, Wyandot, Tuscarora, Meherrin and Nottoway.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Iroquoian languages are polysynthetic and head-marking, and they are known for their general lack of labial consonants. Where labials occur they are largely restricted to borrowings, expressive vocabulary or variant pronunciations. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>, native vocabulary has them only in the words for water and salt. No member contrasts voicing, and most have oral vowels with no nasal counterparts and nasal vowels with no oral ones. Cherokee has lexical tone, which its relatives lack. Attempts to link Iroquoian, Siouan and Caddoan languages in a Macro-Siouan family are suggestive but remain unproven.</p><p>Several of the peoples are poorly documented. The languages of the Wenro, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a> or Conestoga Confederacy, the Neutral Nation and the Erie Nation are very poorly documented in print, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wyandot">Wyandot</a> called the Neutral Atiwandaronk, meaning they who understand the language. By 1660 all of these peoples except the Conestoga and the Haudenosaunee Confederacy had been defeated and scattered, migrating to form new tribes or adopted into others. The Iroquoian peoples adopted valiant enemies, and captive women and children, to replace the dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Almost all surviving Iroquoian languages are severely or critically endangered, and some have only a few elderly speakers. The two languages with the most speakers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> and North Carolina, are spoken by fewer than 10 per cent of the populations of their nations. Programmes support revival: Syracuse University has a Certificate in Iroquois Linguistics for Language Learners, Six Nations Polytechnic in Ohsweken, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> offers diplomas and degrees in Mohawk or Cayuga, and from September 2017 the University of Waterloo has offered a credit course in Mohawk.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iroquoian languages” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tuscarora lived on the coastal plain of North Carolina along the Roanoke, Tar and Neuse rivers, an <b>Iroquoian</b>-speaking people among Algonquian and Siouan neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuscarora-nation">Tuscarora Nation</a></li></ul></section>
